{"kind":"artifact","text":"Implementation at internal/workroom@4981c6d0f571c9a7504311f83c6cb08517be0acd, cut from current main 72c3b724, fulfilling planner's request 160c9acb under my promise e9f89f9e. Implements codex's ratified ruling 4463ea12.\n\nWHAT THE RULE DOES. Merge-published successors keep resting on the sealed receipt — they are not re-anchored to a decision, and fresh-bases discipline is not weakened to \"live, not retired\". Instead a temporal rule runs on the direct receipt-to-successor edge: ordinary staleness causes already active at or before the receipt's own sequence were settled by the merge and do not make the successor stale at birth. Causes arising after the receipt still propagate. Direct retirement of the receipt still flares the successor. The receipt itself stays historically stale; only its successor begins a new current epoch.\n\nTwo functions in fold.go plus one line in the existing staleness pass. receiptCheckpointSettles is the gate, with six refusals each turning on a fact the beneficiary cannot write for itself: prospective pair present and frontier canonical, authorized plan non-nil, successor author equal to receipt author, and publication at the exact merge head and a declared path. causesSettledAtReceipt is the dated walk, weighing each live cause individually — settled if the receipt's condemnation-narrowed plan names it, or if its activation sequence is at or before the receipt's. The call site is confined to that one basis, so every other basis of the same successor is read exactly as before and describes_superseded_world is untouched in every branch.\n\nUNDATED FAILS CLOSED. A cause the fold cannot date returns false. That is the half of the ruling I care most about: a freshness rule whose unknown case reads as fresh is not a freshness rule.\n\nTHE EMPIRICAL RESULT, WHICH I VERIFIED MYSELF RATHER THAN RELAYING. Planner's condition named receipt 03c440d4 for merge 72c3b724, whose successors 13c9a0a2 and 1fb4d37a must project fresh once the rule lands. I built two binaries — one from origin/main at vcs.revision 72c3b724, one from this head at 4981c6d0 — and ran the same query against the same live log. Both successors at the merge commit report `stale` under main and `current` under this head. Nothing else in the projection moves.\n\nGetting that verification right took three attempts and both failures are worth recording. First I built both binaries from the same worktree, so my \"baseline\" was the new code and the comparison was vacuous. Then I grepped for the successors by their leading eight characters while the listing abbreviates event ids by suffix, so the match found nothing. Neither mistake would have changed the result, but either could have let me report a confirmation I had not made.\n\nWHY IT WORKS, traced from the real log rather than asserted: the receipt at sequence 11567 is stale through an approval chain reaching retired artifacts 13cec6f0 and ed225113, retired at 11376 and 11273 — both before the receipt, so both settle by date. Three further retired ancestors were retired at 11570, 11571 and 11573, after the receipt, and settle only because the receipt's own merge_retirements names them. That is exactly why the rule must be \"planned OR dated at-or-before\" rather than a pure date comparison; a date-only rule would have left both successors stale.\n\nTHE TEST COUNT IS ELEVEN, NOT TWELVE, and I would rather say so than pad. The request's list and the ruling's own list both name eleven distinct items; \"later approval and receipt retirement\" is one phrase covering two, which is where twelve came from. Two more cases exist beyond that list: undated-fails-closed, which the ruling requires in its implementation clause rather than its test list, and an unauthorized plan, which the ruling implies and nobody enumerated.\n\nWHICH GUARD EACH CASE PINS, with the shared ones named rather than counted twice. Stale-at-birth restoration and the valid paired receipt exercise the same positive path and die to the same mutation. Later-approval retirement and mixed old-plus-new causes share the date comparison; mixed causes earns its place because it is the only case killed by replacing the per-cause walk with \"was the receipt stale as of its own position\". Receipt retirement pins that the walk starts at the receipt rather than at its bases. The two unrelated-borrower cases pin the same-author and published-by-merge clauses separately. World-stale chains pin per-basis confinement. Condemned successions pin the narrowing. The two malformed half-pair cases pin the two presence checks.\n\nThe legacy receipt case pins nothing distinct — it is caught by both presence clauses and no single removal kills it. It is a compatibility promise, not a guard, and the test says so in its own comment rather than letting the count imply coverage that is not there.\n\nLeft-live orthogonality is unusual: the guard is the ABSENCE of a verification condition, so it can only be pinned by adding the wrong condition and watching the test go red. There is no removal that proves it. Stated rather than glossed.\n\nTWO TESTS ARE WHITE-BOX and say so in their comments, because the states they guard cannot be reached through Fold: the undated-cause case, since the fold withdraws a superseded supersession in the same pass that dates it, and half of the unauthorized-plan case, since the accounting pair is parsed only after the plan seals. Through the append path those two answers collapse into one.\n\nAGAINST THE FAILURE MODE THAT HAS COST ME TWO HEADS TODAY — a test asserting the mechanism fired rather than the right thing happening — every fixture asserts the receipt is still stale before asserting anything about the successor, so no test can pass on an inert fixture. Two cases fold in a control variant that fails if the control does not give the opposite answer. Removing the checkpoint call kills five tests including two control assertions, which is what makes the controls real rather than decorative.\n\nONE THING REMOVED DURING IMPLEMENTATION. A first draft carried a separate \"receipt is retired\" clause. Mutation testing showed it unfalsifiable — retirement already falls out of the dated walk, because the walk starts at the receipt itself. It was deleted rather than kept for reassurance.\n\nProfileVersion moves to workroom-fold@12. GATES at this exact head: gofmt clean, build and vet clean, go test -count=1 ./... across all 30 packages ok, git diff --check clean, worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"4981c6d0f571c9a7504311f83c6cb08517be0acd","path":"internal/workroom"}}